The issue you're encountering is likely due to the fact that the `left` CSS property is not being applied because the element does not have an initial position set. By default, elements are positioned relatively (`position: relative`) unless specified otherwise.

To fix this, you need to ensure that the element has a position that allows the `left` property to take effect. You can do this by setting the `position` property of the element to `relative`, `absolute`, or `fixed`.

Here's how you can modify your code:

### HTML
```html
<div id="game">
    <img id="asd" src="http://i.imgur.com/ApuSWMC.png" alt="torch">
</div>
```

### CSS (Add this inside a `<style>` tag or in an external stylesheet)
```css
#asd {
    position: relative; /* This ensures the left property works */
}
```

### JS
```javascript
$(document).ready(function () {
    $('#asd').click(function () {
        $('#asd').animate({
            left: "+=100px"
        }, 'fast');
    });
});
```

### Explanation:
- **CSS**: The `position: relative;` style ensures that the `left` property will work on the `#asd` element.
- **JavaScript**: The jQuery `.animate()` function is used to animate the `left` property of the `#asd` element when it is clicked.

With these changes, clicking the image should cause it to move 100 pixels to the right. If you want the image to continue moving indefinitely, you would need to adjust the animation logic accordingly.
